# Nginx deployment

This target serves the built Vite SPA with Nginx {{nginxImage}} and keeps runtime configuration outside the JavaScript bundle.

## Build and package

```bash
pnpm build
docker build -f deploy/nginx/Dockerfile -t {{packageName}}:local .
```

Choose one API mode at container runtime.

### Same-origin `/api` reverse proxy

```bash
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 \
  -e APP_ENVIRONMENT=production \
  -e API_BASE_URL=/api \
  -e API_UPSTREAM=http://backend:8080 \
  {{packageName}}:local
```

`API_UPSTREAM` preserves the `/api` prefix. The upstream must expose that public path.

### Static frontend with an absolute API URL

```bash
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 \
  -e APP_ENVIRONMENT=production \
  -e API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com \
  {{packageName}}:local
```

In this mode Nginx does not proxy API traffic. Configure CORS on the API as appropriate.

## Delivery guarantees

- `/healthz` is independent of the API and reports whether the frontend server is alive.
- `/runtime-config.json` is `no-store`/`no-cache` so one image can be configured per environment.
- `/assets/` is cached for one year with `immutable`; Vite filenames are content hashed.
- `index.html` is revalidated and BrowserRouter routes fall back through `try_files`.
- API proxy caching is not enabled by default.
- TLS/HSTS and application-specific CSP are expected to be configured at the owning edge/gateway because generic values can break legitimate embedding or CSS-in-JS behavior.

For strict supply-chain environments, pin the Nginx container image by digest in CI even though the generated Dockerfile pins a stable version tag.
